  ok\ F.6.2.0825  BUFFER: - the "127" literals below need DECIMAL, but a
  ok\ bare DECIMAL with no restore leaks the base change forward into
  ok\ whatever section file loads next (MARKER only resets the
  ok\ dictionary/HERE, never BASE) - save/restore locally instead, so
  ok\ this file keeps the "load in any order" promise its own header
  ok\ comment makes.
  ok\ BUG FIX (round 1): was "BASE @ >R DECIMAL ... R> BASE !" - >R/R>
  ok\ are unsafe spanning separate top-level lines in this subroutine-
  ok\ threaded system (the return stack IS the hardware call stack; >R
  ok\ tucks its value underneath its own return address, which only
  ok\ stays correct while control never returns past that point - but
  ok\ QLOOP returns from INTERPRET after every sent line, so by the time
  ok\ R> runs, many lines later, the stashed value sits where some long-
  ok\ since-returned call frame's return address used to be, and
  ok\ whichever RTS reaches it first jumps into garbage memory).
  ok\ BUG FIX (round 2): switched to "BASE @ DECIMAL ... BASE !" (plain
  ok\ data stack, no >R/R>) - safe from the above, but still fragile in
  ok\ a different way: an UNCAUGHT error anywhere in between resets the
  ok\ whole data stack to empty (QLOOP's own top-level recovery), wiping
  ok\ the parked value, so a later BASE ! would restore garbage. This
  ok\ file itself doesn't throw uncaught errors, but the save/restore
  ok\ pattern shouldn't rely on that file by file. Unconditional restore
  ok\ instead - no save needed, since ambient base entering this block
  ok\ is always HEX by this corpus's own convention (set once by
  ok\ 00_test_prelude.fs and kept correct by every file's own habit of
  ok\ restoring it) - so just set it back to HEX directly, immune to
  ok\ anything that happens to the stack in between.
  okDECIMAL
